  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 345 PM CDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nLow clouds have been stubborn to burn off today thanks to weak\nmixing withing the low level high pressure axis. This high\npressure is shifting further E and allowing for gradually\nincreasing S/SE low level flow and decreasing clouds. Humidity\nis beginning to increase once again (dew points back into he mid\nto upper 60s), but should still be a seasonably pleasant evening\ngiven the light breeze and temperatures in the upper 70s to\nlower 80s through sunset.\n\nExpect another round of stratus and potentially more fog late\ntonight and into Friday AM with return of downright muggy\nconditions and dew points in the low to mid 70s. Bufkit\nsoundings and model progs seem to favor this moisture advection\nbeing more stratus than fog, which makes sense given the steady\n5-10 MPH Srly winds tonight. Models were a little overdone on\nthe fog last night and are even less excited about it tonight,\nso have mentioned patchy fog in the grids, but left out any\nmention of dense fog in the latest HWO.\n\nMain story for the weekend will be return of warm/hot and humid\nconditions - but there's also one or two, albeit highly\nuncertain, chances for thunderstorms to deal with, as well. Dome\nof upper level high pressure will build/expand over the central\nand southern Plains and allow for temperatures to soar into the\n90s, to even low 100s in north central Kansas, each afternoon\nSaturday and Sunday. The warmer KS zones will also see overnight\nlows creep back up into mid 70s. Still can't rule out the need\nfor a heat advisory for some of these areas, especially towards\nSun-Mon time frame, after moisture has had more time to pool\nalong a weak frontal zone. These areas will likely see peak heat\nindices near the 105F criteria, and the warmer overnight lows\nwill add to the \"accumulative effects\" that come with high heat.\nOn the other hand, it's now early August and we're well\naccustomed to the heat that was even higher a couple weeks ago.\nWill continue to monitor trends for any signs of an upward tick\nin dew points or air temperatures and potential need for a heat\nheadline - again, mainly along and S of the NE/KS state line.\n\nSeveral weak waves rounding the edge of the upper level high\ncould lead to some sporadic shower and thunderstorm chances,\nmainly in the Saturday to Saturday night time frame. CAMs are\nquite inconsistent with details, however - differing\nsignificantly on where and when the best chances will be. Some\nguidance points towards mainly a Saturday AM chance, whereas\nother models weaken the AM activity before it gets to us and\nputs the highest chances with potential redevelopment in the \nevening and overnight hours. Instability probably won't be a\nlimiting factor regardless of scenario, and region will be on\nthe southern fringes of greater deep layer shear...so at least\nsome mention of severe weather potential seems warranted. Just\nreally difficult to pin point more specific details at this\ntime, unfortunately.\n\nModels still support the idea of gradually cooling temperatures\nand more active weather pattern for roughly the second half of\nnext week. Temperatures should dip back down into primarily 80s\nfor Neb zones by Wednesday, then area wide for Thursday. The\nupper ridge will break down some and reorient from the Desert SW\ninto more of the Deep South, which should allow for a steady\nstream of monsoonal mid to upper level moisture to stream into\nthe region and interact with frequent shortwaves embedded within\nzonal to NW upper flow. Far too early for much in the way of\ndetails, but broadly speaking, the pattern should favor an\nincrease in shower and thunderstorm potential, likely on a daily\nbasis...and this is confirmed in recent ensemble precip anomaly\nplots for next week. Hopefully this pans out, as many areas\ncould use some moisture now, but especially next week after the\nhot, and mainly dry, weekend.",
